# {{.Name}}

A Go library scaffolded with [Forge](https://github.com/reliant-labs/forge).

## Layout

```
{{.Name}}/
├── pkg/{{.Name}}/  # Public API (importable as {{.Module}}/pkg/{{.Name}})
├── internal/        # Private implementation packages
├── docs/            # Architecture Decision Records
├── forge.yaml       # Forge project manifest (kind: library)
├── go.mod
└── Taskfile.yml
```

## Quick start

```bash
task deps   # go mod download
task test   # go test -race ./..., recorded with its coverage
task lint   # golangci-lint run
task fmt    # goimports + go mod tidy
```

## Test results and coverage

`task test` records every lane it runs, with its coverage, under
`.forge/tests/runs/` (gitignored); `task test:report` summarises the last
run. `FORGE_TESTS=off` records nothing for one run.

CI's "Report test results" step uploads the run to your project's control
plane with no secret: its job has `id-token: write`, and forge exchanges the
job's GitHub OIDC token for a short-lived `tests:write` token. That works once
the Reliant GitHub App is installed on this repository and an org admin has
linked the installation to the org in the web app's organization settings
(Reliant cloud: https://forge.reliantlabs.io). Until then, and on a fork's pull
request, which gets no OIDC token, the step prints one line and stays green; it
never decides the job's result. To upload with a token instead, store one with
`tests:write` (or `deploy:write`) as the secret `FORGE_CONTROL_PLANE_TOKEN` and
pass it in the step's `env:`: the secret, if set, overrides OIDC.
`tests.upload: never` in `forge.yaml` turns upload off.

## Adding code

- **Public API** lives under `pkg/{{.Name}}/`. Anything you want
  importers to use goes here.
- **Private helpers** live under `internal/`. Use
  `forge scaffold package <name>` to scaffold a new internal package with
  a contract interface and a generated mock.
- **Tests** live next to the code (`xxx_test.go`). Runnable examples
  go into `Example*` test functions; they show up rendered in
  generated docs and run as part of `go test`.

## Versioning

This module follows [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org). Cut
releases by tagging the repository:

```bash
git tag v0.1.0
git push origin v0.1.0
```

Importers will pull the tagged version via `go get {{.Module}}@v0.1.0`.
